statement:
Let $f:X\to Y$ be a morphism of schemes and $\mathcal L,\mathcal M$ invertible $\mathcal O_Y$-modules [def-sc-invertible-module]. For $y\in Y$ write $\mathcal L(y)=\mathcal L_y\otimes_{\mathcal O_{Y,y}}\kappa(y)$ for the fibre (a one-dimensional $\kappa(y)$-vector space) and $s(y)\in\mathcal L(y)$ for the value of a section $s$. Here, for an open $V\subseteq Y$ and $s\in\mathcal L(V)$, $f^*s\in(f^*\mathcal L)(f^{-1}V)$ [def-pc-module-pullback] is the image of $s$ under the unit $\mathcal L\to f_*f^*\mathcal L$ of the adjunction $f^*\dashv f_*$, and for $s\in\mathcal L(V)$, $t\in\mathcal M(V)$, $s\otimes t\in(\mathcal L\otimes_{\mathcal O_Y}\mathcal M)(V)$ is the image of $s\otimes t$ under the sheafification map of the tensor product [def-pur-sheaf-tensor-product]; $\kappa(y)$ is the residue field [def-weil-residue-field]. Then:
(1) $f^*\mathcal L$ is an invertible $\mathcal O_X$-module, and $\mathcal L\otimes_{\mathcal O_Y}\mathcal M$ is an invertible $\mathcal O_Y$-module;
(2) there is an isomorphism $f^*(\mathcal L\otimes\mathcal M)\cong f^*\mathcal L\otimes f^*\mathcal M$, functorial in $\mathcal L,\mathcal M$, sending $f^*(s\otimes t)$ to $f^*s\otimes f^*t$; for $g:W\to X$ there is a canonical isomorphism $g^*f^*\mathcal L\cong(f\circ g)^*\mathcal L$ sending $g^*f^*s$ to $(f\circ g)^*s$; and the symmetry $\mathcal L\otimes\mathcal M\cong\mathcal M\otimes\mathcal L$, $s\otimes t\mapsto t\otimes s$, is an isomorphism (so a finite tensor product of invertible modules may be reordered by any permutation of its factors);
(3) for $x\in X$, $(f^*\mathcal L)(x)=\mathcal L(f(x))\otimes_{\kappa(f(x))}\kappa(x)$ with $(f^*s)(x)=s(f(x))\otimes1$, and for $y\in Y$, $(\mathcal L\otimes\mathcal M)(y)=\mathcal L(y)\otimes_{\kappa(y)}\mathcal M(y)$ with $(s\otimes t)(y)=s(y)\otimes t(y)$.
